{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794593677027,"sessionID":"ses_e29e5c0b9ffed1u4GCgcaPeWZq","part":{"id":"prt_1d61e5ee3001OanrovBoy3g7lx","sessionID":"ses_e29e5c0b9ffed1u4GCgcaPeWZq","messageID":"msg_1d61e5525001QeWSFsPT01dxqQ","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794593691106,"sessionID":"ses_e29e5c0b9ffed1u4GCgcaPeWZq","part":{"partID":"prt_1d61e8fda0015Ng8QEC0gBTPpg","sessionID":"ses_e29e5c0b9ffed1u4GCgcaPeWZq","messageID":"msg_1d61e5525001QeWSFsPT01dxqQ","type":"tool","id":"call_f9ae342be1ae4101afd018b9","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"rg -n \"BACKUP_FALLBACK_DIR|BACKUP_NAME_CHARS\" .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/agents-md-embed.cjs | head; echo ===; rg -n \"AdvisorRecommendationSchema\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md"},"output":"76:const BACKUP_FALLBACK_DIR = 'doctor-agents-md';\n80:const BACKUP_NAME_CHARS = 16;\n955:  return path.join(base, digest.slice(0, BACKUP_NAME_CHARS));\n982:  return { dir: path.join(os.tmpdir(), BACKUP_FALLBACK_DIR), fallback: true, reason };\n===\n31:Each recommendation carries an optional `compiledRoute` field (the `compiledRoute` field of `AdvisorRecommendationSchema` in `schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts`, `z.record(z.string(), z.unknown()).optional()`). `handlers/advisor-recommend.ts`'s `enrichCompiledRoutes()` attaches it only when at least one recommended `skillId` is both in the fixed 7-hub `COMPILED_ROUTING_HUBS` set and currently permitted by the tri-state `SPECKIT_COMPILED_ROUTING` flag (`compiledRoutingEnabledForHub()`, from `../lib/compiled-routing-flag.ts`). Otherwise the schema re-parse is skipped entirely and the output returns unchanged. When enrichment runs, `compiledRouteForRecommendation()` shells out to `.skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub <skillId> --prompt-stdin`, writes the prompt to its stdin and attaches the parsed result. The one exception is a result whose `servingAuthority` is `\"legacy\"`: the field then stays absent, because a legacy sentinel is never surfaced as if it were a compiled decision. A subprocess failure is handled the same way: silently no `compiledRoute` field, with an optional debug-gated stderr breadcrumb via `SPECKIT_COMPILED_ROUTING_DEBUG`, so a probe failure degrades to exactly what an ineligible or flag-off recommendation already looks like. In short, `compiledRoute` is attached only for an eligible, flag-permitted hub that is genuinely serving compiled. It is intentionally absent for every other combination: flag off, hub not in `COMPILED_ROUTING_HUBS`, legacy sentinel or probe failure. See [`feature-flag-governance.md`](../../../system-spec-kit/feature-catalog/governance/feature-flag-governance.md) for the flag's phased-default and serving-status contract, and each eligible hub's own `compiled-routing-and-legacy-fallback.md` catalog leaf for the hub-side directive this enrichment mirrors. The front door runs from the request's `workspaceRoot`, and a caller may name another checkout there, such as a worktree under the repository root. So the handler first checks that the front door it would run reads `--prompt-stdin`. An older front door gets no call and the recommendation carries no `compiledRoute`, because it would route an empty prompt.\n33:When a recommendation gets a `compiledRoute`, `enrichCompiledRoutes()` also asks `attachCommand()` in `lib/command-attach.ts` for a slash command and sets the optional `command` field when one comes back (the `command` field of `AdvisorRecommendationSchema` in `schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts`, `z.string().min(1).optional()`). `attachCommand()` names a command only for a `route` decision with `selectionKind: 'single'` and exactly one target, and only when the generated `MODE_COMMANDS` table maps that hub and `workflowMode` to a command. A bundle, a multi-target route or a mode with no command leaves `command` off. For `system-deep-loop` the prompt must type the same command or hold a phrase from `DEPTH_WORDING`, so a prompt that only names a topic such as research or review gets no deep-loop command. A prompt that matches `NO_EDIT_REQUEST` gets no command from any hub, because a reader who rules out a change is never handed work that writes.\n43:Each recommendation carries up to four optional fields that let a reader act on it without a second lookup. `kind` is `'skill'` or `'command'` and says which face the pick is, so a caller can tell a skill entry from a command row that scored as a candidate. `command` is the runnable slash string, such as `/speckit:save`. `policy` is `'run'`, `'start'` or `'ask'` and says how a caller may run that command. `ownerSkillId` names the skill that owns the command, which is the grouping key when several commands belong to one hub. All four stay optional in `AdvisorRecommendationSchema` so a response from a runtime that predates them still parses.\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"76:const BACKUP_FALLBACK_DIR = 'doctor-agents-md';\n80:const BACKUP_NAME_CHARS = 16;\n955:  return path.join(base, digest.slice(0, BACKUP_NAME_CHARS));\n982:  return { dir: path.join(os.tmpdir(), BACKUP_FALLBACK_DIR), fallback: true, reason };\n===\n31:Each recommendation carries an optional `compiledRoute` field (the `compiledRoute` field of `AdvisorRecommendationSchema` in `schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts`, `z.record(z.string(), z.unknown()).optional()`). `handlers/advisor-recommend.ts`'s `enrichCompiledRoutes()` attaches it only when at least one recommended `skillId` is both in the fixed 7-hub `COMPILED_ROUTING_HUBS` set and currently permitted by the tri-state `SPECKIT_COMPILED_ROUTING` flag (`compiledRoutingEnabledForHub()`, from `../lib/compiled-routing-flag.ts`). Otherwise the schema re-parse is skipped entirely and the output returns unchanged. When enrichment runs, `compiledRouteForRecommendation()` shells out to `.skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub <skillId> --prompt-stdin`, writes the prompt to its stdin and attaches the parsed result. The one exception is a result whose `servingAuthority` is `\"legacy\"`: the field then stays absent, because a legacy sentinel is never surfaced as if it were a compiled decision. A subprocess failure is handled the same way: silently no `compiledRoute` field, with an optional debug-gated stderr breadcrumb via `SPECKIT_COMPILED_ROUTING_DEBUG`, so a probe failure degrades to exactly what an ineligible or flag-off recommendation already looks like. In short, `compiledRoute` is attached only for an eligible, flag-permitted hub that is genuinely serving compiled. It is intentionally absent for every other combination: flag off, hub not in `COMPILED_ROUTING_HUBS`, legacy sentinel or probe failure. See [`feature-flag-governance.md`](../../../system-spec-kit/feature-catalog/governance/feature-flag-governance.md) for the flag's phased-default and serving-status contract, and each eligible hub's own `compiled-routing-and-legacy-fallback.md` catalog leaf for the hub-side directive this enrichment mirrors. The front door runs from the request's `workspaceRoot`, and a caller may name another checkout there, such as a worktree under the repository root. So the handler first checks that the front door it would run reads `--prompt-stdin`. An older front door gets no call and the recommendation carries no `compiledRoute`, because it would route an empty prompt.\n33:When a recommendation gets a `compiledRoute`, `enrichCompiledRoutes()` also asks `attachCommand()` in `lib/command-attach.ts` for a slash command and sets the optional `command` field when one comes back (the `command` field of `AdvisorRecommendationSchema` in `schemas/advisor-tool-schemas.ts`, `z.string().min(1).optional()`). `attachCommand()` names a command only for a `route` decision with `selectionKind: 'single'` and exactly one target, and only when the generated `MODE_COMMANDS` table maps that hub and `workflowMode` to a command. A bundle, a multi-target route or a mode with no command leaves `command` off. For `system-deep-loop` the prompt must type the same command or hold a phrase from `DEPTH_WORDING`, so a prompt that only names a topic such as research or review gets no deep-loop command. A prompt that matches `NO_EDIT_REQUEST` gets no command from any hub, because a reader who rules out a change is never handed work that writes.\n43:Each recommendation carries up to four optional fields that let a reader act on it without a second lookup. `kind` is `'skill'` or `'command'` and says which face the pick is, so a caller can tell a skill entry from a command row that scored as a candidate. `command` is the runnable slash string, such as `/speckit:save`. `policy` is `'run'`, `'start'` or `'ask'` and says how a caller may run that command. `ownerSkillId` names the skill that owns the command, which is the grouping key when several commands belong to one hub. All four stay optional in `AdvisorRecommendationSchema` so a response from a runtime that predates them still parses.\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"rg -n \\\"BACKUP_FALLBACK_DIR|BACKUP_NAME_CHARS\\\" .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/agents-md-embed.cjs | head; echo ===; rg -n \\\"AdvisorRecommendationSchema\\\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/feature-catalog/cli-surface/advisor-recommend.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794593689562,"end":1794593691106}}}}
